WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.318
For least squares we learned

2
00:00:05.404 --> 00:00:07.217
the normal equations

3
00:00:07.217 --> 00:00:10.844
A transpose A x hat equals A transpose b.

4
00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:12.398
They are correct.

5
00:00:12.398 --> 00:00:15.420
But are they safe to compute with?

6
00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:19.174
Take a matrix with stretches four and one

7
00:00:19.255 --> 00:00:21.281
its ellipse is four times

8
00:00:21.362 --> 00:00:23.226
longer than it is wide.

9
00:00:23.226 --> 00:00:26.792
What happens to the shape of A transpose A?

10
00:00:26.792 --> 00:00:28.170
Pause and think.

11
00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.008
A transpose A has the squared

12
00:00:34.086 --> 00:00:36.656
singular values: sixteen and one.

13
00:00:36.656 --> 00:00:38.680
Drawn to the same length

14
00:00:38.758 --> 00:00:40.939
its ellipse is sixteen times

15
00:00:41.017 --> 00:00:42.340
longer than wide.

16
00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.910
The condition number is squared.

17
00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:48.727
So the normal equations, correct on paper

18
00:00:48.727 --> 00:00:51.368
can be far worse in a computer.

19
00:00:51.368 --> 00:00:54.587
A problem with kappa ten to the eight

20
00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:57.063
harmless in double precision

21
00:00:57.063 --> 00:01:00.117
becomes one with ten to the sixteen

22
00:01:00.117 --> 00:01:02.180
and every digit is lost.

23
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:03.997
Here it happens.

24
00:01:03.997 --> 00:01:06.547
Three equations in two unknowns

25
00:01:06.547 --> 00:01:10.026
with a tiny epsilon, ten to the minus eight

26
00:01:10.026 --> 00:01:13.040
built so the exact answer is one, one.

27
00:01:13.118 --> 00:01:16.055
The condition number of T is about one

28
00:01:16.132 --> 00:01:18.760
point four times ten to the eight.

29
00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:22.093
T transpose T has one plus epsilon

30
00:01:22.181 --> 00:01:24.287
squared on the diagonal.

31
00:01:24.374 --> 00:01:26.655
But epsilon squared is ten

32
00:01:26.743 --> 00:01:28.586
to the minus sixteen

33
00:01:28.586 --> 00:01:32.183
and one plus that rounds to exactly one.

34
00:01:32.183 --> 00:01:35.780
The stored matrix is all ones: singular.

35
00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:38.324
It squashes the circle flat.

36
00:01:38.412 --> 00:01:41.308
The small entries are lost before

37
00:01:41.395 --> 00:01:43.150
solving even starts.

38
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.420
A solver that works on T itself

39
00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.519
through the SVD or QR

40
00:01:48.519 --> 00:01:52.444
never forms that product, and returns one

41
00:01:52.444 --> 00:01:52.944
one.

42
00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.506
The same trouble appears in regression.

43
00:01:56.506 --> 00:01:58.290
Fifty made-up samples

44
00:01:58.368 --> 00:02:00.928
with a second feature that is the

45
00:02:01.006 --> 00:02:03.255
first plus one percent noise.

46
00:02:03.333 --> 00:02:05.505
The condition number of X is

47
00:02:05.583 --> 00:02:07.600
about two hundred fifteen.

48
00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:11.363
Fit the weights, with a little noise in y.

49
00:02:11.363 --> 00:02:13.963
Fit again with different noise

50
00:02:13.963 --> 00:02:15.425
the weights jump.

51
00:02:15.425 --> 00:02:18.350
And again: they jump the other way.

52
00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.728
But look where they land

53
00:02:20.809 --> 00:02:23.000
all three on the line w one

54
00:02:23.081 --> 00:02:24.866
plus w two equals two.

55
00:02:24.866 --> 00:02:28.355
The data pins down the sum, the long axis.

56
00:02:28.436 --> 00:02:30.302
It hardly says anything

57
00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:33.385
about the difference, the short axis

58
00:02:33.385 --> 00:02:35.900
so the weights slide along it.

59
00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:37.792
Ridge regression

60
00:02:37.792 --> 00:02:39.953
with lambda zero point one

61
00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:42.269
raises the denominator of the

62
00:02:42.346 --> 00:02:44.198
smallest singular value.

63
00:02:44.198 --> 00:02:47.440
Fit the same three noisy data sets again.

64
00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:51.463
Now all three answers stay close to one

65
00:02:51.463 --> 00:02:51.922
one.

66
00:02:51.922 --> 00:02:56.054
Standardising features, merging near copies

67
00:02:56.054 --> 00:02:59.910
and ridge all lower the condition number.

68
00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:02.800
Machine learning often computes

69
00:03:02.875 --> 00:03:04.290
in lower precision.

70
00:03:04.365 --> 00:03:06.228
Take the two nearly equal

71
00:03:06.302 --> 00:03:07.793
lines from part one.

72
00:03:07.793 --> 00:03:10.848
In single precision, about seven digits

73
00:03:10.848 --> 00:03:13.754
the answer is off in the fourth digit

74
00:03:13.754 --> 00:03:15.170
as kappa predicts.

75
00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:18.653
In half precision, about three digits

76
00:03:18.653 --> 00:03:21.457
even storing the data changes it.

77
00:03:21.539 --> 00:03:24.425
One point zero zero one becomes one

78
00:03:24.507 --> 00:03:27.146
point zero zero zero nine eight

79
00:03:27.228 --> 00:03:30.114
and two point zero zero one becomes

80
00:03:30.197 --> 00:03:33.000
two point zero zero one nine five.

81
00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:35.197
The exact solution of the

82
00:03:35.271 --> 00:03:37.266
stored system is zero, two.

83
00:03:37.266 --> 00:03:39.188
Once the data is rounded

84
00:03:39.188 --> 00:03:42.365
no careful solver can bring back one, one.

85
00:03:42.439 --> 00:03:45.912
This is also why mixed precision training keeps

86
00:03:45.986 --> 00:03:48.868
a single precision copy of the weights

87
00:03:48.942 --> 00:03:51.306
tiny updates would simply vanish

88
00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:52.710
in half precision.

89
00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:55.691
A check for working with AI.

90
00:03:55.691 --> 00:03:58.007
Suppose an assistant says

91
00:03:58.007 --> 00:04:00.665
the determinant of M is small

92
00:04:00.751 --> 00:04:03.066
so multiply M by a thousand

93
00:04:03.152 --> 00:04:05.210
to fix the conditioning.

94
00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:09.171
The determinant does grow, to one thousand.

95
00:04:09.171 --> 00:04:12.866
But every stretch grows by the same factor

96
00:04:12.866 --> 00:04:16.142
so kappa is still about four thousand.

97
00:04:16.226 --> 00:04:19.333
And the same change in the last digit

98
00:04:19.417 --> 00:04:22.440
still sends the answer to zero, two.

99
00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:25.035
Rescaling changes nothing.

100
00:04:25.035 --> 00:04:27.539
To help, change the problem

101
00:04:27.539 --> 00:04:29.611
better features, ridge

102
00:04:29.611 --> 00:04:31.510
or more precise data.

103
00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:35.088
So: solve least squares on A itself

104
00:04:35.088 --> 00:04:37.086
never on A transpose A

105
00:04:37.086 --> 00:04:40.084
which squares the condition number.

106
00:04:40.084 --> 00:04:42.748
With nearly collinear features

107
00:04:42.831 --> 00:04:45.079
the data pins down the long

108
00:04:45.163 --> 00:04:48.410
axis of the weights, not the short one.

109
00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:50.038
Next lecture

110
00:04:50.038 --> 00:04:53.480
solving huge systems step by step

111
00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:55.840
with iterative methods.
